      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "**Clinical Case Study: Patient Assessment of Suspected Fractured Femur**\n\n*Presenting Situation:*\nArriving at the Emergency Room, the patient, a 35-year-old male, is accompanied by his wife. As he's assisted onto the stretcher, the clinical team immediately assesses his pain and vital signs.\n\n*Vital Signs:*\n- Blood Pressure: 110/70 mmHg\n- Heart Rate: 102 bpm (palpitations noted)\n- Respiratory Rate: 18 per minute (slightly labored)\n- Temperature: 98.4°F (37°C)\n\n*Pain Assessment (Qualitative):*\nThe patient reports severe, sharp pain in his left leg, rating it as an 8 on a scale of 0-10, and illustrates with a trembling hand. He rates it as even higher when asked about neck pain.\n\n*Pain Assessment (Quantitative):*\nUtilizing the Wong-Baker FACES Pain Rating Scale, the patient marks his on the face picture where he could see a forced smile corresponding to a \"6.\"\n\n*Nervous System Assessment:*\nHe displays increased anxiety and appears slightly disoriented when asked about the location of his leg. His pupils are reactive but slightly larger than normal",
